\chapter{Overdispersion}

If you remember one chapter from this book, make it this one. Overdispersion is
the default state of real count data, and ignoring it produces confident,
publishable, wrong conclusions.

\section{What it is}

The Poisson model insists that
\[
\Var(Y \mid x) = \E(Y \mid x) = \mu.
\]
\textbf{Overdispersion} means the data are more variable than that:
\[
\Var(Y \mid x) = \phi \mu, \qquad \phi > 1.
\]
$\phi$ is the \emph{dispersion parameter}. If $\phi = 1$ the Poisson is fine. If
$\phi = 3$, your data bounce around three times as much as the Poisson allows.

\begin{plainenglish}[The everyday version]
The Poisson assumes that once you know the average, you know exactly how much the
counts wobble. Real data almost always wobble more, because there is something
going on that you have not measured.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{Why it happens}

Five common causes, in rough order of frequency:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Unobserved heterogeneity.} Individuals differ in ways you did not
        record. Some people are simply accident-prone. Mixing many different
        Poisson rates together produces something more variable than any single
        Poisson.
  \item \textbf{Clustering / correlation.} Repeated measures on the same subject,
        pupils in classes, chicks in broods. Non-independence inflates variance.
  \item \textbf{Contagion.} One event makes another more likely. Infections,
        traffic jams, cascading equipment failures.
  \item \textbf{Excess zeros.} A subgroup that structurally cannot have the event
        at all (Chapter 10).
  \item \textbf{Model misspecification.} A missing predictor, a missing
        interaction, or a non-linear effect fitted as linear. This one is worth
        ruling out \emph{before} you reach for a fancier distribution.
\end{enumerate}

Here is cause 1, shown directly. Mix Poissons with different rates and watch the
variance overshoot the mean.

<<why-overdisp, fig.width=9, fig.height=3.8, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Both panels have the same overall mean. On the left every unit shares one rate, so variance equals mean. On the right the rate varies between units, and the resulting mixture has variance far above its mean.">>=
set.seed(3)
N <- 5000
y_pois <- rpois(N, lambda = 4)                       # everyone identical
lam    <- rgamma(N, shape = 0.7, rate = 0.7/4)       # rates vary between units
y_mix  <- rpois(N, lambda = lam)                     # a gamma-Poisson mixture = NB

par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
h1 <- table(factor(y_pois, levels = 0:25))
barplot(h1 / N, col = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.75), border = "white",
        xlab = "count", ylab = "proportion",
        main = sprintf("One shared rate\nmean %.2f, variance %.2f",
                       mean(y_pois), var(y_pois)))
h2 <- table(factor(y_mix, levels = 0:25))
barplot(h2 / N, col = adjustcolor(PAL["accent"], 0.75), border = "white",
        xlab = "count", ylab = "proportion",
        main = sprintf("Rates differ between units\nmean %.2f, variance %.2f",
                       mean(y_mix), var(y_mix)))
@

Notice the right-hand panel has more zeros \emph{and} a much longer tail, at the
same average. That shape --- extra zeros plus a long tail --- is the signature of
overdispersion.

\begin{keyidea}
A mixture of Poissons whose rates follow a gamma distribution \emph{is} a negative
binomial. That is not a coincidence or an analogy; it is the mathematical origin of
the negative binomial, and the reason it is the natural repair for unobserved
heterogeneity.
\end{keyidea}

\section{Why it matters: a simulation}

Overdispersion barely moves the coefficients. It wrecks the standard errors. Here
is the damage, measured.

<<overdisp-damage, fig.width=9, fig.height=3.8, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="1000 simulated datasets with NO true effect of x. The Poisson model's z-statistics are far too spread out, so it rejects the null far too often. The quasi-Poisson model is calibrated.">>=
set.seed(99)
sim_one <- function(n = 120, theta = 1) {
  x  <- rnorm(n)
  mu <- exp(1.5 + 0 * x)                       # TRUE effect of x is exactly zero
  y  <- rnbinom(n, mu = mu, size = theta)      # but the data are overdispersed
  mp <- glm(y ~ x, family = poisson)
  mq <- glm(y ~ x, family = quasipoisson)
  c(pois = coef(summary(mp))["x", "z value"],
    quasi = coef(summary(mq))["x", "t value"])
}
res <- t(replicate(1000, sim_one()))

## How often does each model declare a significant effect that does not exist?
round(c(poisson_type1_error = mean(abs(res[, "pois"])  > 1.96),
        quasi_type1_error   = mean(abs(res[, "quasi"]) > 1.96),
        nominal             = 0.05), 3)

par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
for (j in 1:2) {
  hist(res[, j], breaks = 40, freq = FALSE, border = "white",
       col = adjustcolor(c(PAL["red"], PAL["green"])[j], 0.6),
       xlim = c(-6, 6), ylim = c(0, 0.45), xlab = "test statistic for x",
       main = c("Poisson: too confident", "Quasi-Poisson: honest")[j])
  curve(dnorm(x), add = TRUE, lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["primary"])
  abline(v = c(-1.96, 1.96), lty = 2, col = PAL["grey"])
}
@

The Poisson model rejects a true null hypothesis about
\Sexpr{round(100*mean(abs(res[, "pois"]) > 1.96))}\% of the time instead of 5\%.
That is not a small distortion. It means roughly
\Sexpr{round(mean(abs(res[, "pois"]) > 1.96)/0.05, 1)} times as many false
discoveries as advertised.

\begin{warningbox}
Overdispersion makes standard errors too small, confidence intervals too narrow,
and $p$-values too small. It almost never biases the coefficients themselves. So a
model that looks great --- everything significant! --- is exactly the pattern
overdispersion produces.
\end{warningbox}

\section{Detecting it}

\subsection{Method 1: the Pearson dispersion statistic}

The workhorse. Compute
\[
\hat\phi = \frac{1}{n-p}\sum_i \frac{(y_i - \hat\mu_i)^2}{\hat\mu_i}
        = \frac{X^2}{\text{residual df}}.
\]

<<quine-fit>>=
data(quine, package = "MASS")
str(quine)
m_pois <- glm(Days ~ Eth + Sex + Age + Lrn, family = poisson, data = quine)

dispersion <- function(model) {
  X2 <- sum(residuals(model, type = "pearson")^2)
  df <- df.residual(model)
  c(pearson_X2 = X2, df = df, dispersion = X2 / df,
    p_value = pchisq(X2, df, lower.tail = FALSE))
}
round(dispersion(m_pois), 4)
@

A dispersion of \Sexpr{round(dispersion(m_pois)["dispersion"], 1)} is severe. The
counts vary about \Sexpr{round(dispersion(m_pois)["dispersion"], 0)} times more
than a Poisson permits, so the standard errors printed by \fn{glm} are too small
by a factor of roughly
$\sqrt{\Sexpr{round(dispersion(m_pois)["dispersion"], 1)}} \approx
\Sexpr{round(sqrt(dispersion(m_pois)["dispersion"]), 1)}$.

\subsection{Method 2: residual deviance over degrees of freedom}

You will see this reported constantly, because it is right there in the
\fn{summary} output.

<<dev-df>>=
c(residual_deviance = deviance(m_pois), df = df.residual(m_pois),
  ratio = deviance(m_pois) / df.residual(m_pois))
@

\begin{warningbox}[Use the Pearson version, not the deviance version]
The deviance/df ratio is convenient but biased, especially when many fitted means
are small (under about 5). It can suggest overdispersion when there is none. The
Pearson statistic is the one with the theory behind it. When they disagree,
believe Pearson.
\end{warningbox}

\subsection{Method 3: a formal test}

\fn{AER::dispersiontest} tests $H_0: \Var = \mu$ against
$\Var = \mu + \alpha\, f(\mu)$.

<<disp-test>>=
library(AER)
dispersiontest(m_pois)                     # tests Var = mu + alpha*mu   (NB1-style)
dispersiontest(m_pois, trafo = 2)          # tests Var = mu + alpha*mu^2 (NB2-style)
@

Both reject decisively. The \fn{trafo = 2} version is the one that corresponds to
the negative binomial you get from \fn{glm.nb}.

\subsection{Method 4: look at it}

Numbers are good; pictures are better. Plot squared residuals against fitted
values, and compare the observed count distribution with what the model expects.

<<overdisp-plots, fig.width=9.4, fig.height=4, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Left: if the Poisson held, the average squared Pearson residual would sit on the horizontal line at 1. Right: the Poisson model badly under-predicts both the zeros and the long tail.">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
pr2 <- residuals(m_pois, type = "pearson")^2
plot(fitted(m_pois), pr2, pch = 21, bg = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.35),
     col = "white", cex = 1.1, log = "y",
     xlab = "fitted mean", ylab = "squared Pearson residual",
     main = "Squared Pearson residuals")
grid_book(); abline(h = 1, lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["green"])
lines(lowess(fitted(m_pois), pr2), lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["red"])
legend("topright", bty = "n", cex = 0.75, lwd = 2.5,
       col = c(PAL["green"], PAL["red"]),
       legend = c("Poisson expects 1", "actual average"))

## Observed vs expected frequency of each count
maxk <- 40
obs  <- table(factor(pmin(quine$Days, maxk), levels = 0:maxk))
expd <- sapply(0:maxk, function(k) sum(dpois(k, fitted(m_pois))))
plot(0:maxk, as.numeric(obs), type = "h", lwd = 3, col = PAL["primary"],
     xlab = "days absent", ylab = "frequency",
     main = "Observed vs Poisson-expected counts")
grid_book()
lines(0:maxk, expd, lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["red"])
points(0:maxk, expd, pch = 19, cex = 0.5, col = PAL["red"])
legend("topright", bty = "n", cex = 0.8, lwd = c(3, 2.5),
       col = c(PAL["primary"], PAL["red"]), legend = c("observed", "Poisson expects"))
@

\subsection{Method 5: the simplest check of all}

Does the model reproduce the number of zeros, and the size of the biggest values?

<<zero-check>>=
c(observed_zeros = sum(quine$Days == 0),
  poisson_expects = round(sum(dpois(0, fitted(m_pois))), 1))
c(observed_max = max(quine$Days),
  poisson_99th_percentile = round(max(qpois(0.99, fitted(m_pois)))))
@

The Poisson expects essentially no zeros and cannot produce values anywhere near
the observed maximum. That is a comprehensive failure.

\section{Rule out the boring explanation first}

Before concluding ``the distribution is wrong'', check that the \emph{mean model}
is right. Overdispersion is often just a missing term.

<<misspec-demo>>=
set.seed(21)
n <- 300
x <- runif(n, 0, 3)
z <- rbinom(n, 1, 0.5)                       # an important variable...
y <- rpois(n, exp(0.4 + 0.6 * x + 1.4 * z))  # ...that really matters

## Leave z out: the data look overdispersed
round(dispersion(glm(y ~ x, family = poisson))["dispersion"], 3)
## Put z in: the "overdispersion" disappears
round(dispersion(glm(y ~ x + z, family = poisson))["dispersion"], 3)
@

The data were generated from a perfect Poisson. Omitting a real predictor
manufactured a dispersion of
\Sexpr{round(dispersion(glm(y ~ x, family=poisson))["dispersion"], 2)} out of
nothing.

\begin{rulebox}[Checklist before switching distributions]
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Have you included all the predictors you have?
  \item Should any continuous predictor be curved (a spline, a log, a quadratic)?
  \item Are there interactions you have ignored?
  \item Do you need an offset for unequal exposure?
  \item Are there a handful of extreme outliers driving everything?
  \item Are the observations clustered?
\end{enumerate}
Only after all six should you conclude the distribution itself is wrong.
\end{rulebox}

\section{Your options once overdispersion is real}

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.24\textwidth}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.42\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Option} & \textbf{\R} & \textbf{When to use it} \\
\midrule
Quasi-Poisson & \fn{family = quasipoisson} & Quick fix; you only want correct SEs.
  Variance $=\phi\mu$. No AIC, no likelihood. \\
Robust (sandwich) SEs & \fn{coeftest(m, vcov = sandwich)} & Same idea, keeps the
  Poisson likelihood. Good with clustering too. \\
Negative binomial & \fn{MASS::glm.nb} & The standard answer. Variance
  $=\mu + \mu^2/\theta$. A real likelihood, so AIC and LRTs work. \\
Observation-level random effect & \fn{glmer(\dots + (1|obs))} & Poisson-lognormal
  mixture; natural inside a GLMM. \\
Zero-inflated / hurdle & \fn{zeroinfl}, \fn{hurdle} & Only if the excess is
  specifically in the zeros. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Underdispersion}

Less common, but it happens: $\hat\phi < 1$, data \emph{less} variable than
Poisson. Typical causes are a bounded or regulated process (a clinic that always
books between 8 and 12 patients), or counts that were rounded or capped.

<<underdisp>>=
set.seed(5)
y_under <- rbinom(200, size = 12, prob = 0.5)      # mean 6, variance only 3
mu_ <- glm(y_under ~ 1, family = poisson)
round(dispersion(mu_)["dispersion"], 3)
@

Consequences run the other way: standard errors are too \emph{large}, so you lose
power but stay conservative. Options are quasi-Poisson (which handles $\phi < 1$
perfectly well), a binomial model if there is a real upper bound, or a
Conway--Maxwell--Poisson model (Chapter 11).

\begin{keyidea}[The one-line summary of this chapter]
Fit the Poisson, compute
\fn{sum(residuals(m, type="pearson")\textasciicircum 2) / df.residual(m)}, and act
on the answer. Near 1: keep it. Above about 1.2: fix it. Above 2: you must fix it.
\end{keyidea}
